    "lawCode": "Amd §19, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S3945\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the tax law, in relation to the green building credit\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nThe current tax credits available for \"green buildings\" are largely\ninaccessible to individual homeowners because of their complexity\nThis legislation simplifies the process by permitting individual\nhomeowners to receive Leadership in Energy & Environmental Design\n(LEED) or National Association of Home Builders (NAHB) certification\nfor green homes and then use that certification to apply for the\nexisting green buildings tax credits.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 - amends the section 19 of the tax law by creating a new\nsubsection (f) which permits homeowners who have homes certified as\nLEED or NAHB green to receive a tax credit under the \"Green Buildings\nTax Credit\". This tax credit will be available to homeowners\nregardless of their annual tax burden.\n\nSection 2 - Effective Date\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nOne of the barriers to green homes is the perceived high cost of\nconstruction and up-front costs. The National Association of\nHomebuilders reports that demand for green homes continues to rise.\nIn 2011, the green market for homes represented about 17% of overall\nmarket share. The overall share of the market is estimated to increase\nanywhere from 29% to 38%. According to NAHB, 61% of costumers are\nwilling to pay a higher cost for new green construction, and 66% of\ncostumers are willing to pay a higher cost for remolded green homes.\n\nIn order to spur the development of green homes in New York, this tax\ncredit will enable homeowners to recoup some of the up-front costs of\n\"greening\" their homes. The benefits to homeowners include lower\nenergy costs, higher property values, and a more sustainable home.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall apply to taxable\nyears beginning on and after January 1, 2015.\n\n\n",
